  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1142 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nA quasi-stationary front remains draped over south/south-central\nMississippi with a shortwave over central Missouri. Trapped\nbetween the air masses, a thick stratus deck will prohibit strong\nradiational cooling with morning lows projected to be in the 60s.\nA reinforcing cold front will begin to sink southeast in the\ncoming hours through the daytime on Sunday. The frontal boundary\nwill provide enough lift ahead of it resulting in a few showers\nalong and south of the I-40 corridor on Sunday. Most of this\nactivity after sunrise will be confined to northeast Mississippi\nand should be clear of the area by sunset. Clearing of skies will\nbe gradual as subsidence occurs behind the reinforcing front.\n\nBy Monday, cooler air will be in place under a continental air\nmass. NBM temperature guidance typically undercuts mixing and max\nheating in a very dry air mass. Temperatures nudging closer to\nthe 75th percentile would not be out of the question for the next\nseveral days as dry air dominates through midweek. The bias on\ntemperatures is a bit concerning with projected dewpoints in the\n40s and 50s resulting in RH values less than 30%.These RH values\ncould lead to elevated fire danger through most of the week. Be\nsure to use caution when burning and heed any local burn bans.\n\nBy midweek, shortwave ridging will emerge and inch temperatures\nupwards into the mid to upper 80s. Temperatures will climb a\ndegree or two higher daily and some areas have low probabilities\n(around 20%) of reaching 90 degrees by the end of the work week.\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance both favor a wet and\nunsettled weather returning late next weekend, but details remain\nuncertain.\n\nDNM",
